{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,60p' docs/reference_factory_talk.md\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: factory-missions-talk-reference\ndescription: Reference material from Factory's \"Assembling Agent Teams\" talk at AI Engineer Europe -- core architecture details for the Missions multi-agent system\ntype: reference\noriginSessionId: d0be7010-3858-4b92-8153-32045af7ab9b\n---\nSource: Talk by Factory's core agent harness lead at AI Engineer Europe (April 2026). Slide images in `/Users/stuart/Downloads/talkimages/` (13 JPEGs). Full transcript and summary provided by Stuart in the initial conversation.\n\n## Key Architecture Details\n\n**Five multi-agent strategies (taxonomy):**\n1. Delegation -- parent spawns sub-agents\n2. Creator-Verifier -- separate agents for building vs checking\n3. Direct Communication -- agents talk without coordinator (fragile)\n4. Negotiation -- agents coordinate over shared resources\n5. Broadcast -- one agent sends info to many\n\n**Missions combine 4 of 5** (delegation, creator-verifier, broadcast, negotiation) into a single workflow.\n\n**Three-role architecture:**\n- **Orchestrators** -- plan features/milestones, ask clarifying questions, produce a \"validation contract\" (defines done before coding starts, can be hundreds of assertions)\n- **Workers** -- implement one feature at a time with clean context, commit to git, structured handoff report (what was done, what's undone, commands/exit codes, issues, procedure compliance)\n- **Validators** -- two types:\n  - *Scrutiny Validator* -- test suite, type checking, lint, dedicated code review agents per feature\n  - *User Testing Validator* -- QA engine that spawns the app, interacts via computer use, fills forms, clicks buttons, checks flows end-to-end\n\n**Execution model:**\n- Serial feature execution (one worker at a time) to avoid conflicts\n- Parallelization only for read-only ops (code search, API research) and within validators\n- Correctness compounds over multi-day runs\n\n**Model strategy:**\n- Model-agnostic: different LLMs for planning (slow reasoning), implementation (fast code fluency), validation (precise instruction following)\n- Structure compensates for non-frontier models\n- Almost all orchestration logic is in ~700 lines of prompts/skills, not hard-coded\n\n**Longest mission:** 16 days. Results: ~50% test coverage lines, ~90% code coverage. Most time spent on user-testing validation, not code generation.\n\n**Designed not to be obsolete:** when a better model drops, the system just gets smarter with no code changes needed.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"tool_end","dispatch_id":"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2","ts":"2026-05-01T13:41:19Z","tool":"command_execution"}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,110p' docs/provenance.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Provenance\n\nHow Darkfactory relates to its two original sources: Factory.ai's multi-agent mission\narchitecture and Matt Pocock's AI development skills. Reviewed against the current\nimplementation (SKILL.md) as of 2026-04-12.\n\n---\n\n## Factory.ai\n\nSource: [docs/reference_factory_talk.md](reference_factory_talk.md)\n\n### Faithfully implemented\n\n| Concept | Factory description | Darkfactory implementation |\n|---------|--------------------|----------------------------|\n| Three-role architecture | Orchestrator, worker, validator as distinct roles | Orchestrator is the /mission session. Workers are dispatched subagents. Validators are separate subagents with isolated prompts. |\n| Validation contract | \"Defines done before coding starts, can be hundreds of assertions\" | contract.md (Phase 5), written after specs/tasks but before the build loop. Workers never see it. |\n| Adversarial separation | Workers don't see the contract, validators don't see the plan | Stricter than Factory: workers never see contract.md, validators never see proposal.md or design.md. |\n| Serial execution | One worker at a time to avoid conflicts | Rule 1: \"One worker at a time. Never dispatch two workers in parallel.\" |\n| Structured handoffs | Workers produce reports with what was done, commands, exit codes, issues | Handoff template: commit SHA, approach, commands run, concerns. |\n| Two validator types | Scrutiny validator (tests, lint, review) and user-testing validator (spawns app, interacts) | Scrutiny (Phase 8 Step 4): build/test/lint/review/standards/simplify/security/assertions/behavioral conformance. User-test (Phase 8 Step 6): HTTP tests or browser. |\n| Multi-model strategy | Different LLMs for different roles | config.yaml: workers default to Codex (GPT-5.4), validators to Claude, adversarial reviewers use one of each. |\n| Read-only parallelization | Only parallelize read-only operations | Only parallel dispatch is the two adversarial reviewers in Phase 9, which are read-only. |\n\n### Implemented differently\n\n| Concept | Factory description | Darkfactory difference |\n|---------|--------------------|-----------------------|\n| User-test validator | \"Computer use\" QA engine that spawns the app and interacts | Uses HTTP tests and browser automation (/test-app). Same concept, less sophisticated execution. |\n\n### Not implemented\n\n| Concept | Factory description | Why not |\n|---------|--------------------|---------| \n| Negotiation pattern | Agents coordinating over shared resources | Darkfactory is purely orchestrator-mediated. No inter-agent coordination. Serial execution eliminates the need for resource negotiation. |\n\n---\n\n## Matt Pocock\n\nSource: [docs/reference_pocock_talk.md](reference_pocock_talk.md)\n\n### Faithfully implemented\n\n| Concept | Pocock description | Darkfactory implementation |\n|---------|-------------------|-----------------------------|\n| Adversarial alignment interview | 40-100 questions to reach shared understanding before any work | Phase 1: /grill. Adds a safeguard Pocock didn't describe: warns if user tries to skip. |\n| Ubiquitous language | DDD-inspired glossary with canonical terms, aliases to avoid, relationships | Phase 2: /language. Glossary threaded through briefs, documentation, and all phases. |\n| TDD as speed limit | Force small steps -- write test, make pass, refactor. Prevents AI from outracing its headlights. | Worker briefs mandate /tdd. Scrutiny validator enforces with automatic FAIL if diff has zero new tests (test presence gate). |\n| Deep modules | Wrap related code in deep modules. Large functionality behind simple interfaces. Test at the interface. | Phase 4.3 (design) requires module depth analysis: describe the interface, what it hides, why that boundary. Flags shallow modules. |\n\n### Implemented differently\n\n| Concept | Pocock description | Darkfactory difference |\n|---------|-------------------|-----------------------|\n| Gray boxes | Treat deep modules as gray boxes. Design the interface, delegate internals to AI. Human selectively reviews internals for critical paths. | Automated via adversarial separation: validators review code without seeing design intent. Human reviews only the final adversarial reports and draft PR. More automated than Pocock's manual approach. |\n| Strategic vs tactical | Include module changes and interface designs in PRDs. Human thinks strategically, AI programs tactically. | Replaced /prd-to-plan with a sequential artifact pipeline (proposal -> specs -> design -> tasks) with human gates at each step. Same goal, different mechanism. |\n\n---\n\n## What Darkfactory adds\n\nConcepts in the current implementation that neither source described:\n\n| Addition | Purpose |\n|----------|---------|\n| Spec coverage audit with deviation classification | Maps every PRD requirement to tasks. Classifies deviations as interpretation, substitution, or removal with escalating approval. |\n| Contract cross-referencing to PRD | Assertions trace to specific PRD requirements. Deviated items are marked. |\n| Fix loop with circuit breaker | On validation failure, spawns a fix worker and re-runs both validators. Caps at 3 attempts before escalating to human. |\n| Spec delta check | Post-build verification that every COVERED item in the coverage audit is actually present in the built code. |\n| Final gates | Whole-repo build/test/lint/format after the last task catches regressions from late tasks. |\n| Behavioral conformance | Scrutiny validator attempts to verify observable behavior by execution, not just code reading. |\n| Dual adversarial review | Two independent engines (Codex + Claude) review the full diff in parallel at mission end. |\n| File-backed state and resume | All decisions persisted to disk. Missions survive session death and can be resumed. |\n| Mandatory closing tasks | Cleanup and documentation tasks after all feature tasks. |\n| Demo video capture | Records frontend functionality after validation passes, for human review. |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"tool_end","dispatch_id":"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2","ts":"2026-05-01T13:41:19Z","tool":"command_execution"}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' docs/design-decisions.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Darkfactory Design Decisions\n\nCaptured from the grill session on 2026-04-10.\n\n## Vision\n\nA lightweight multi-agent mission system built entirely from Claude Code primitives (skills, subagents, plan mode, hooks) and Codex CLI. No custom IDE or app. Inspired by Factory's Missions architecture.\n\n## Phase Flow\n\n```\nalign -> spec -> contract -> build -> validate -> ship\n```\n\nInner loop per task: `spec -> build -> verify -> ship` with repetition on validation failure.\n\n## Architecture\n\n### Orchestrator\n- IS the main Claude Code session (Opus 4.6, not configurable -- it's the session)\n- Enters plan mode at start -- the plan is the durable operating manual that survives compaction\n- Interactive during planning (grills the user, proposes plan, gets approval)\n- Autonomous during execution (runs the worker/validator loop)\n- Manages all state via files on disk\n- Spawns workers via Codex, validators via Claude subagents\n\n### Workers\n- One worker per task (vertical slice pattern)\n- Receive a brief file -- self-contained, includes task + proposal excerpt + design excerpt + procedures\n- Do NOT receive the contract (adversarial separation)\n- Do NOT receive previous workers' handoff reports (clean context)\n- Commit all work in a single commit\n- Write a structured handoff report to disk\n- Default engine: Codex (configurable in mission/config.yaml)\n\n### Per-task validators (run sequentially)\n1. **Scrutiny validator** -- test presence gate, build/test/lint, /review, code standards, /simplify, /security (conditional), contract assertions. Always runs.\n2. **User-test validator** -- decides API tests (HTTP) and/or browser tests (agent-browser) based on what changed. Always runs.\n3. **Demo video** -- recorded after both validators pass, for frontend tasks. Orchestrator decides when.\n\n### End-of-mission review\n4. **Dual adversarial review** -- runs once after the draft PR is created, against the full diff. Codex and Claude independently review the code. Both reports are presented to the human. No automated fix loop -- the human decides what to address.\n\nValidators receive: commit SHA + contract assertions. They do NOT receive proposal, design, or worker reasoning.\n\n### Fix Loop\n- On validation failure: orchestrator writes a symptom-based fix brief (no contract text) to mission/issues/, spawns a fix worker\n- Fix worker uses `/tdd` to fix\n- Fix worker commits a single fix commit\n- Both per-task validators re-run after every fix (scrutiny + user-test)\n- Circuit breaker: 3 attempts on the same task -> pause and ask human\n- Skipped tasks marked with `- [~]` in tasks.md -- the build loop ignores them\n\n## Key Design Decisions\n\n### Bash run_in_background for external processes\n- Long-running external processes (Codex workers, Codex adversarial reviews) run via Bash with `run_in_background: true`, with stdout+stderr redirected to a log file under `mission/logs/`\n- Claude subagent validators use the Agent tool directly since they run within the session\n- The log file lets the human watch progress in another terminal (`tail -f`) without the agent paying context cost for mid-run events\n\n### File-backed state\n- All mission state lives on disk in the mission directory\n- The orchestrator reads fresh state before each cycle (survives compaction)\n- Enables session resume if Claude Code dies\n- tmux as first line of defense against session death\n\n### Serial execution\n- One worker at a time (matches Factory's approach)\n- Parallelization only for read-only operations within validators\n- Correctness compounds over time\n\n### Vertical slices\n- Each task in tasks.md should be a thin vertical slice cutting through all layers\n- A completed task is demoable and verifiable on its own\n- One task = one worker = one commit = one validation cycle\n\n### Configurable engines\n- mission/config.yaml specifies which engine per role (codex/claude)\n- Default: Codex for workers and adversarial reviewer A, Claude for validators, adversarial reviewer B, and demo video\n- Swap engines when new models drop without changing anything else\n\n### OpenSpec integration\n- `/mission` is the single workflow for all work (small fixes to large epics). Scope adapts naturally.\n- contract.md is a new OpenSpec artifact (validation assertions, written during planning)\n- mission/ directory nests inside openspec/changes/<change-name>/\n- Archiving the change archives the mission artifacts too\n\n### Entry point and completion\n- `/mission` skill starts a mission\n- `/mission-resume` resumes from file state\n- `/mission-status` shows progress\n- On completion: draft PR created via `gh pr create --draft`\n- Human reviews and promotes to ready\n\n## Orchestrator Plan Structure\n\n```markdown\n# Mission: [change-name]\n\n## State Directory\nopenspec/changes/<change-name>/mission/\n\n## OpenSpec Artifacts\n- Proposal: openspec/changes/<change-name>/proposal.md\n- Design: openspec/changes/<change-name>/design.md\n- Tasks: openspec/changes/<change-name>/tasks.md\n- Contract: openspec/changes/<change-name>/contract.md\n\n## Engine Config\n- Workers: codex\n- Scrutiny validator: claude\n- User-test validator: claude\n- Adversarial reviewer A: codex\n- Adversarial reviewer B: claude\n- Demo video: claude\n\n## Skills to Use\n- Alignment: /grill (during planning, before artifacts are created)\n- Shared language: /language (during planning, establish terms)\n- PRD: /prd (turn grill conversation into structured PRD)\n- Planning: /opsx:propose (create proposal, design, tasks from PRD)\n- Task breakdown: each task is a vertical slice through all layers, demoable and verifiable on its own\n- Contract: written by orchestrator after planning, before workers start\n- Worker implementation: /tdd (test-driven development for workers)\n- Worker briefs: assembled from proposal.md + design.md + task description\n- Scrutiny validation: build/test/lint + /review + /simplify against contract\n- Security validation: /security (for tasks touching auth/sensitive paths)\n- User-test validation: /test-app (agent-browser) and/or HTTP tests against API\n- Adversarial review: dual Codex + Claude review in parallel, orchestrator synthesizes\n- Demo video: /demo-video (for frontend tasks)\n- Completion: gh pr create --draft (draft PR with mission summary)\n- Archival: /opsx:archive (after merge)\n\n## Mission Flow\nFor each unchecked task in tasks.md:\n1. Read mission state from mission/progress.md\n2. Write brief to mission/briefs/task-N.md\n3. Kick off worker via Bash `run_in_background` (engine per config.yaml)\n4. On completion, read mission/handoffs/task-N.md\n5. Spawn scrutiny validator (commit SHA + contract assertions)\n6. If fail -> write symptom-based fix brief, spawn fix worker, re-validate both. Circuit breaker: 3 attempts -> ask human.\n7. Spawn user-test validator (commit SHA + contract assertions)\n8. If fail -> same fix/re-validate loop\n9. If frontend task -> spawn demo video\n10. Mark task complete in tasks.md, update progress.md\n\nAfter all tasks complete:\n11. Create draft PR\n12. Run dual adversarial review (Codex + Claude) against full diff\n13. Present both reports to human alongside PR link\n\n## Rules\n- One worker at a time (serial execution)\n- Workers never see contract.md\n- Validators never see proposal.md or design.md\n- On any failure, re-run BOTH validators after fix\n- Write all decisions to progress.md\n```\n\n## Mission Directory Structure\n\n```\nopenspec/changes/<change-name>/\n  .openspec.yaml\n  proposal.md\n  design.md\n  tasks.md\n  contract.md\n  mission/\n    config.yaml\n    progress.md\n    briefs/\n      task-1.md\n      task-2.md\n    handoffs/\n      task-1.md\n      task-2.md\n    validations/\n      task-1-scrutiny.md\n      task-1-usertest.md\n      task-1-demo.mp4\n    issues/\n      task-1-v1.md\n```\n\n## Worker Brief Format\n\n```markdown\n# Worker Brief: Task N - [name]\n\n## Your Task\n[Task description and acceptance criteria]\n\n## Project Context\n[Relevant excerpt from proposal.md]\n\n## Architecture\n[Relevant excerpt from design.md]\n\n## Procedures\n- Implement the task as described\n- Commit all changes in a single commit with message: \"[task-N] description\"\n- Write your handoff report to mission/handoffs/task-N.md using the format below\n\n## Handoff Report Format\n- Task: [which task]\n- Commit SHA: [the SHA]\n- Approach: [brief explanation of what you did and why]\n- Commands run: [build/test/lint commands and exit codes]\n- Concerns: [anything you're uncertain about]\n```\n\n## Contract Format\n\n```markdown\n# Contract\n\n## Task 1: [name]\n- [Assertion verifiable from outside without knowing the code]\n- [Another assertion]\n\n## Task 2: [name]\n- [Assertion]\n```\n\n## Fix Brief Format\n\n```markdown\n# Fix Brief: Task N - [name] (Attempt 2)\n\n## What Failed\n[Specific failed assertions from the validator report]\n\n## Validator Output\n[Relevant error messages, test failures, review findings]\n\n## Commit to Fix\n[SHA of the broken commit]\n\n## Procedures\n- Fix only the issues described above\n- Do not refactor or change anything unrelated\n- Commit as a single commit with message: \"[task-N] fix: description\"\n- Write handoff report to mission/handoffs/task-N-fix-v2.md\n```\n\n## Build Order (Darkfactory itself)\n\n1. **Orchestrator skeleton** -- /mission skill, plan mode, grill, /opsx:propose, contract.md, mission directory\n2. **Worker loop** -- brief writing, Codex invocation via Bash `run_in_background`, handoff reading\n3. **Scrutiny validation** -- build/test/lint + /review + fix loop with circuit breaker\n4. **User-test validation** -- agent-browser + HTTP tests\n5. **Demo video + draft PR** -- completion phase\n6. **Resume + status** -- /mission-resume, /mission-status skill\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"tool_end","dispatch_id":"01KQHWAF8YKGC3W4RXTFKRP3G2","ts":"2026-05-01T13:41:19Z","tool":"command_execution"}
