{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_27","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/provenance.md | sed -n '14,75p'\"","aggregated_output":"    14\t\n    15\t| Concept | Factory description | Darkfactory implementation |\n    16\t|---------|--------------------|----------------------------|\n    17\t| Three-role architecture | Orchestrator, worker, validator as distinct roles | Orchestrator is the /mission session. Workers are dispatched subagents. Validators are separate subagents with isolated prompts. |\n    18\t| Validation contract | \"Defines done before coding starts, can be hundreds of assertions\" | contract.md (Phase 5), written after specs/tasks but before the build loop. Workers never see it. |\n    19\t| Adversarial separation | Workers don't see the contract, validators don't see the plan | Stricter than Factory: workers never see contract.md, validators never see proposal.md or design.md. |\n    20\t| Serial execution | One worker at a time to avoid conflicts | Rule 1: \"One worker at a time. Never dispatch two workers in parallel.\" |\n    21\t| Structured handoffs | Workers produce reports with what was done, commands, exit codes, issues | Handoff template: commit SHA, approach, commands run, concerns. |\n    22\t| Two validator types | Scrutiny validator (tests, lint, review) and user-testing validator (spawns app, interacts) | Scrutiny (Phase 8 Step 4): build/test/lint/review/standards/simplify/security/assertions/behavioral conformance. User-test (Phase 8 Step 6): HTTP tests or browser. |\n    23\t| Multi-model strategy | Different LLMs for different roles | config.yaml: workers default to Codex (GPT-5.4), validators to Claude, adversarial reviewers use one of each. |\n    24\t| Read-only parallelization | Only parallelize read-only operations | Only parallel dispatch is the two adversarial reviewers in Phase 9, which are read-only. |\n    25\t\n    26\t### Implemented differently\n    27\t\n    28\t| Concept | Factory description | Darkfactory difference |\n    29\t|---------|--------------------|-----------------------|\n    30\t| User-test validator | \"Computer use\" QA engine that spawns the app and interacts | Uses HTTP tests and browser automation (/test-app). Same concept, less sophisticated execution. |\n    31\t\n    32\t### Not implemented\n    33\t\n    34\t| Concept | Factory description | Why not |\n    35\t|---------|--------------------|---------| \n    36\t| Negotiation pattern | Agents coordinating over shared resources | Darkfactory is purely orchestrator-mediated. No inter-agent coordination. Serial execution eliminates the need for resource negotiation. |\n    37\t\n    38\t---\n    39\t\n    40\t## Matt Pocock\n    41\t\n    42\tSource: [docs/reference_pocock_talk.md](reference_pocock_talk.md)\n    43\t\n    44\t### Faithfully implemented\n    45\t\n    46\t| Concept | Pocock description | Darkfactory implementation |\n    47\t|---------|-------------------|-----------------------------|\n    48\t| Adversarial alignment interview | 40-100 questions to reach shared understanding before any work | Phase 1: /grill. Adds a safeguard Pocock didn't describe: warns if user tries to skip. |\n    49\t| Ubiquitous language | DDD-inspired glossary with canonical terms, aliases to avoid, relationships | Phase 2: /language. Glossary threaded through briefs, documentation, and all phases. |\n    50\t| TDD as speed limit | Force small steps -- write test, make pass, refactor. Prevents AI from outracing its headlights. | Worker briefs mandate /tdd. Scrutiny validator enforces with automatic FAIL if diff has zero new tests (test presence gate). |\n    51\t| Deep modules | Wrap related code in deep modules. Large functionality behind simple interfaces. Test at the interface. | Phase 4.3 (design) requires module depth analysis: describe the interface, what it hides, why that boundary. Flags shallow modules. |\n    52\t\n    53\t### Implemented differently\n    54\t\n    55\t| Concept | Pocock description | Darkfactory difference |\n    56\t|---------|-------------------|-----------------------|\n    57\t| Gray boxes | Treat deep modules as gray boxes. Design the interface, delegate internals to AI. Human selectively reviews internals for critical paths. | Automated via adversarial separation: validators review code without seeing design intent. Human reviews only the final adversarial reports and draft PR. More automated than Pocock's manual approach. |\n    58\t| Strategic vs tactical | Include module changes and interface designs in PRDs. Human thinks strategically, AI programs tactically. | Replaced /prd-to-plan with a sequential artifact pipeline (proposal -> specs -> design -> tasks) with human gates at each step. Same goal, different mechanism. |\n    59\t\n    60\t---\n    61\t\n    62\t## What Darkfactory adds\n    63\t\n    64\tConcepts in the current implementation that neither source described:\n    65\t\n    66\t| Addition | Purpose |\n    67\t|----------|---------|\n    68\t| Spec coverage audit with deviation classification | Maps every PRD requirement to tasks. Classifies deviations as interpretation, substitution, or removal with escalating approval. |\n    69\t| Contract cross-referencing to PRD | Assertions trace to specific PRD requirements. Deviated items are marked. |\n    70\t| Fix loop with circuit breaker | On validation failure, spawns a fix worker and re-runs both validators. Caps at 3 attempts before escalating to human. |\n    71\t| Spec delta check | Post-build verification that every COVERED item in the coverage audit is actually present in the built code. |\n    72\t| Final gates | Whole-repo build/test/lint/format after the last task catches regressions from late tasks. |\n    73\t| Behavioral conformance | Scrutiny validator attempts to verify observable behavior by execution, not just code reading. |\n    74\t| Dual adversarial review | Two independent engines (Codex + Claude) review the full diff in parallel at mission end. |\n    75\t| File-backed state and resume | All decisions persisted to disk. Missions survive session death and can be resumed. |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
